--TEST--
The four ways a `Core\Attributes` retrieval is refused where it is written (`rule:attributes/structural-retrieval` and `rule:attributes/retrieval-folds-while-checking`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
type Route = {path: string};

class Limits {
    public const int MAX = 5;
    // A constant whose own declaration folds to no value: `rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants` inlines a
    // class constant at every use site, and this compiler does not fold one
    // constant into another. That gap is `E0792` at a read, and it is the
    // whole of what `E0731` is left reporting below.
    public const int RELAY = Limits::MAX;
}

#[Route(path: "/a")]
#[Route(path: "/b")]
class Twice {}

// Attached to a class of its own, retrieved once: the payload of a site is
// type-checked once per retrieval that reaches it, so a second retrieval here
// would be a second copy of both diagnostics rather than a second mistake.
#[{code: Limits::RELAY}]
class Relay {}

// Accepted first, so a position that stops being accepted fails here rather
// than as a missing refusal: `all<T>` is what two matches are for.
array<Route> $ok = Core\Attributes::all<Route>(Twice::constructor(...));
echo Core\Arr::count($ok) as string, "\n";

// § 5: `get` answers at most one, and two is decided here rather than by a
// test run.
?Route $ambiguous = Core\Attributes::get<Route>(Twice::constructor(...));

// § 4: retrieval is structural, so `T` has to be a shape.
?int $scalar = Core\Attributes::get<int>(Twice::constructor(...));

// § 4: the target is a first-class-callable reference, inspected where it is
// written — a closure value names no declaration.
callable $fn = fn (): int => 1;
?Route $computed = Core\Attributes::get<Route>($fn);

// § 5: the payload satisfies the shape, and the class constant it holds has no
// value of its own to compile in — the constant folder's gap surfacing at the
// one site that needs the value rather than the name.
?{code: mixed} $unfoldable = Core\Attributes::get<{code: mixed}>(Relay::constructor(...));

// § 4: a *written* member name is checked against the target's real
// declarations. It has to be — the `null` a misspelling folds to is the answer
// a correct retrieval of an absent attribute gives, so nothing downstream
// could tell them apart. Only a computed name falls back to that empty result.
?Route $misspelled = Core\Attributes::get<Route>(Twice::constructor(...), "path");
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0792]: `Limits::RELAY` has no compile-time value to inline
   --> case.nvs:20:10
   |
20 | #[{code: Limits::RELAY}]
   |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this constant's declaration folds to no value
   = help: `rule:classes/no-free-functions-or-constants` inlines a class constant at every use site, so its value has to have a constant form — a literal, or an array literal of them. Another class's constant, an enum case and `Foo::class` are the three this compiler cannot yet fold into one, written on their own or nested inside a container: write the value out here, or move it to a `static` member the class initializes

error[E0731]: this attribute satisfies the retrieved shape, but holds a value with no compile-time form
   --> case.nvs:20:10
   |
20 | #[{code: Limits::RELAY}]
   |          ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ no constant form
   = help: `rule:attributes/retrieval-folds-while-checking` replaces the retrieval with the payload itself, so every value in it has to be materializable — a class constant, `Foo::class` and an enum case all are, so what is left is a constant whose own declaration folds to nothing (`E0792`): write the value out here, or give that constant a foldable one

error[E0728]: `Twice::constructor` carries 2 attached literals satisfying this shape
   --> case.nvs:30:21
   |
30 | ?Route $ambiguous = Core\Attributes::get<Route>(Twice::constructor(...));
   |                     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ `get` answers at most one
   = help: `rule:attributes/retrieval-folds-while-checking`: an attached-attribute list is static, so this is decided here rather than by a test run — write `Core\Attributes::all<T>(…)`, which answers every match

error[E0729]: `Core\Attributes::get` retrieves a shape, and `int` is not one
   --> case.nvs:33:16
   |
33 | ?int $scalar = Core\Attributes::get<int>(Twice::constructor(...));
   |                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ `int` written here
   = help: `rule:attributes/structural-retrieval`: retrieval is structural — an attached literal is an answer exactly when it satisfies `T` under `rule:types/shape-type`'s width subtyping, so `T` is an inline `{...}` or a `type` alias naming one

error[E0730]: a `Core\Attributes` retrieval names no declaration
   --> case.nvs:38:48
   |
38 | ?Route $computed = Core\Attributes::get<Route>($fn);
   |                                                ^^^ this is not a declaration reference
   = help: `rule:attributes/structural-retrieval`: the target is written as a first-class-callable reference and inspected where it is written — `Foo::bar(...)` for a method, and `Foo::constructor(...)` for the class itself, plus a literal member name for one of its properties or parameters

error[E0798]: a `Core\Attributes` retrieval names `$path`, which `Twice` does not declare
   --> case.nvs:49:75
   |
49 | ?Route $misspelled = Core\Attributes::get<Route>(Twice::constructor(...), "path");
   |                                                                           ^^^^^^ no parameter or property of that name
   = help: `rule:attributes/structural-retrieval`: a *written* member name is checked against the target's real declarations here, because the answer a misspelling would fold to — `null`, or the empty array — is the same one a correct retrieval of an absent attribute gives, and nothing later can tell them apart; only a computed `$member` falls back to that empty result

error: aborting due to 6 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
